00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:06.260
Sentinel-1 radar image shows Buenos Aires seasons through this multi-month composite approach.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:11.240
If you stacked every Sentinel-1 radar image of Earth taken in a single year,

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:15.660
the stack would be taller than Mount Everest yet still missed nothing on the surface.

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:21.120
The satellite sends radio pulses downward and records the strength and timing of the echo that

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:27.160
bounces back, a process that works day or night and through clouds. In the Buenos Aires composite,

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:33.940
January data were assigned blue, March green, and May red. Any patch that change between

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:40.060
acquisitions lights up in the resulting color mix. Agricultural fields therefore shift from green

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:46.320
to red as crops grow and then dry, while the city stays gray because buildings do not move.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:51.220
The same technique can measure millimeter-scale ground motion after earthquakes,

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:58.080
